<h2>The Zero FIR under Section 173 BNSS</h2>
<p>Section 173 BNSS requires that information relating to the commission of a cognizable offence be recorded <em>irrespective of the area where the offence is committed</em>. The provision also permits information to be given by electronic communication, to be taken on record on signature within three days. What practice long called a Zero FIR is therefore now anchored in the statute itself: the police station first approached cannot turn the informant away on the ground of territory. It registers the information, assigns it the number zero, and forwards it to the police station having jurisdiction, which re-registers it under a regular number and takes up the investigation.</p>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><h4>1. Information recorded</h4><p>Any police station records the information of a cognizable offence, regardless of where the offence occurred, as a Zero FIR.</p></div>
<div class="fstep"><h4>2. Transfer</h4><p>The Zero FIR is transmitted to the police station within whose jurisdiction the offence is alleged to have been committed — in the same district, another district, or another State.</p></div>
<div class="fstep"><h4>3. Re-registration</h4><p>The receiving police station registers the case under its own FIR number and the investigation proceeds there; the Zero FIR number falls away for most practical purposes.</p></div>
<div class="fstep"><h4>4. Bail forum follows</h4><p>Courts exercising jurisdiction over the investigating police station become the natural forum for anticipatory bail, remand and related proceedings.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Where to seek anticipatory bail</h2>
<p>Section 482 BNSS empowers the High Court or the Court of Session to direct that a person apprehending arrest for a non-bailable offence be released on bail in the event of arrest. When an FIR has travelled, the application is ordinarily made before the Court of Session or the High Court having jurisdiction over the police station that is actually investigating the case — after transfer and re-registration, that means the receiving State, not the State where the Zero FIR happened to be recorded. Filing in the wrong forum wastes the most precious commodity in these matters, time, and an order from a court without connection to the investigation is of doubtful utility to the investigating agency expected to honour it.</p>
<p>A distinct, limited practice deserves one paragraph and no more, because this site carries a separate explainer on it. Where arrest appears imminent and the applicant cannot immediately reach the courts of the investigating State, courts in the applicant’s home State are in practice sometimes moved for short <strong>transit or interim protection</strong>: time-bound protection intended only to let the applicant travel and move the competent court. It is an exceptional, discretionary accommodation, usually granted on strict conditions and for a defined period; it is not a substitute for a regular application under Section 482 BNSS before the court connected to the investigation, and this explainer proceeds on the footing that the regular application remains the destination.</p>

<h2>Recurring practical situations</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><h4>Duplicate FIRs in two States</h4><p>A Zero FIR in one State and a regular FIR in another, or parallel complaints by different relatives, can produce two live FIRs on overlapping facts. Protection obtained in one does not extend to the other, so each must be identified by number and police station and dealt with on its own.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h4>Matrimonial complaints at the parental home city</h4><p>Complaints arising from a matrimonial relationship are frequently lodged in the city where the wife’s parental home is situated, even when the matrimonial home lies elsewhere. The husband’s family then faces an investigation, and a bail forum, far from their residence — a scenario where the Zero FIR and transfer framework, and the question of the correct forum, matter acutely.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h4>Verifying FIR status</h4><p>Before filing, counsel should verify on the State police citizen portal whether an FIR exists, whether it is a Zero FIR awaiting transfer, and whether re-registration has occurred, since the application must correctly describe the FIR number and police station. Portals withhold copies in sensitive categories, in which case an application to the police station or the court becomes necessary.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Conditions that must work across State lines</h2>
<p>When protection is granted in a transferred or inter-State matter, the conditions attached under Section 482 BNSS have to be workable across distance. The standard conditions — making oneself available for interrogation as required, not influencing witnesses, not leaving India without leave — take on a practical dimension: courts often direct the applicant to appear before the investigating officer of the receiving police station on specified dates, or permit first appearance through counsel with physical appearance to follow. An applicant residing in another State should propose concrete, verifiable arrangements: dates of appearance, an undertaking to respond to notices under the Sanhita at a disclosed address and telephone number, and, where the court permits, joining the investigation by video-conferencing for preliminary steps. Compliance must then be documented, because in a cross-State matter the status report of a distant investigating officer is often the only account of conduct the court will see.</p>

<h2>Keeping the record straight</h2>
<p>Transferred investigations punish loose drafting. The application should state the history in one clean sequence: the Zero FIR number, date and police station; the transfer; the re-registered FIR number and the investigating police station; and the provision-wise offences alleged, with BNS sections and, where useful, their IPC equivalents. Annexing whatever portal printouts or certified copies exist saves the first hearing from being consumed by questions about which FIR the court is actually dealing with.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>In Zero FIR and transferred matters, the single most consequential early decision is the choice of forum. Identifying the police station actually investigating, and moving the court connected to it with a complete paper trail of the FIR’s journey, usually matters more than any argument on merits at the first hearing.</p>
</div>
